This guide walks through setting up a lightweight self-hosted server stack using Docker on Ubuntu.

The stack includes:

Jellyfin media server

Minecraft Java server

Ollama local AI server

OpenWebUI for interacting with the AI

The setup is designed for low-power hardware.

Tested Hardware

This setup was tested on:

Component	Specification
Device	Intel NUC
CPU	Intel Core i5 (4 cores)
RAM	8 GB
OS	Ubuntu 24.04
Performance Results

Based on testing with the above hardware:

Jellyfin

2–3 simultaneous 1080p streams worked without issues.

Minecraft Server

The server runs smoothly with a small number of players.

A Jellyfin stream can run at the same time without problems.

If multiple Jellyfin streams and Minecraft are active, performance may degrade.

Ollama AI

This guide uses the TinyLlama model.

The model runs but responses are slow on CPU.

For practical usage more RAM would be recommended.

Larger models such as Qwen can be used but require additional memory.

For this setup the AI server is mainly a proof of concept.

Operating System

This guide uses Ubuntu 24.04 Desktop.

Ubuntu Server will also work, however commands may differ slightly on other operating systems.

Note

If you are storing media or AI datasets on external drives you must mount them manually and update the Docker volume paths accordingly.

1. Install Ubuntu

Install Ubuntu 24.04 Desktop or Server.

During installation:

Use default partitioning

Create a user account

Ensure network connectivity

After installation update the system:

sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade -y
2. Install Docker

Install Docker and the Compose plugin.

sudo apt install docker.io docker-compose-plugin -y

Start and enable Docker:

sudo systemctl enable docker
sudo systemctl start docker

Allow your user to run Docker without sudo:

sudo usermod -aG docker $USER

Apply the change:

newgrp docker

Verify Docker is working:

docker ps
3. Create the Project Directory

Create a directory to store the configuration.

mkdir ~/services
cd ~/services
4. Create the Docker Compose Configuration

Create the compose file:

nano docker-compose.yml

Paste the following configuration:

services:

  jellyfin:
    image: jellyfin/jellyfin
    container_name: jellyfin
    ports:
      - "8096:8096"
    volumes:
      - ./jellyfin/config:/config
      - ./media:/media
    restart: unless-stopped
    mem_limit: 1g
    cpus: "1"

  minecraft:
    image: itzg/minecraft-server
    container_name: minecraft
    ports:
      - "25565:25565"
    volumes:
      - ./minecraft:/data
    environment:
      - EULA=TRUE
      - MEMORY=2G
    restart: unless-stopped
    mem_limit: 3g
    cpus: "2"

  ollama:
    image: ollama/ollama
    container_name: ollama
    ports:
      - "11434:11434"
    volumes:
      - ./ollama:/root/.ollama
    restart: unless-stopped
    mem_limit: 1g
    cpus: "1"

  openwebui:
    image: ghcr.io/open-webui/open-webui:main
    container_name: openwebui
    ports:
      - "3000:8080"
    environment:
      - OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://ollama:11434
    restart: unless-stopped
    mem_limit: 1g
    cpus: "0.75"

Save and exit:

CTRL + O
CTRL + X
5. Start All Services

Launch the containers:

docker compose up -d

Verify they are running:

docker ps

Expected containers:

jellyfin
minecraft
ollama
openwebui
6. Access the Services

Find your server IP:

ip a

Example:

192.168.1.50
Jellyfin

Open in a browser:

http://SERVER-IP:8096

Follow the setup wizard and configure your media libraries.

OpenWebUI (AI Interface)

Open:

http://SERVER-IP:3000

This provides a web interface for interacting with Ollama models.

Minecraft Server

Connect from Minecraft Java Edition using:

SERVER-IP:25565
7. Install a Lightweight AI Model

Install TinyLlama:

docker exec -it ollama ollama pull tinyllama

Verify installation:

docker exec -it ollama ollama list

Example output:

NAME
tinyllama

Refresh the OpenWebUI page and select the model.

8. Folder Structure

After setup your directory will look like this:

services/
├── docker-compose.yml
├── jellyfin/
│   └── config/
├── minecraft/
├── ollama/
└── media/

Place media files inside the media directory.

9. Managing the Server

Start services:

docker compose up -d

Stop services:

docker compose down

View logs:

docker logs CONTAINER_NAME

Example:

docker logs minecraft
10. Resource Allocation

Recommended memory usage for an 8 GB system:

Service	RAM
Minecraft	~3 GB
Jellyfin	~1 GB
Ollama	~1 GB
OpenWebUI	~1 GB

This leaves enough memory for the operating system.

11. Optional: External Storage

For larger media libraries or AI datasets you may want to use external drives.

Example mount point:

/media/server/Elements

Update the compose file volume mapping accordingly:

/media/server/Elements:/media
Optional: Remote Access with Tailscale

You may also consider installing Tailscale VPN to access your services remotely.

Tailscale allows secure access to your server from anywhere with an internet connection.

Result:

You now have a lightweight homelab providing:

Media streaming with Jellyfin

A Minecraft multiplayer server

A local AI assistant running entirely offline

All services run inside Docker containers and can be managed from a single compose file.
